 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 1.06 — Conics and Quadrics\n\nA **conic** is the zero set of a general quadratic in two variables,\n\n$$Q(x,y) = a_{11}x^2 + 2a_{12}xy + a_{22}y^2 + 2b_1 x + 2b_2 y + c = 0,$$\n\nand a **quadric** is the same thing one dimension up. The classical treatment — completing squares, memorising discriminants — obscures what is really happening. In matrix form,\n\n$$Q(x) = x^{\\mathsf T}\\!A\\,x + 2b^{\\mathsf T}x + c, \\qquad A = A^{\\mathsf T},$$\n\nand the entire classification is a statement about the **eigenvalues of $A$**. Nothing else is needed.\n\nThe reason is the **spectral theorem**: a real symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalised, $A = R^{\\mathsf T}\\Lambda R$ with $R$ a rotation. So after rotating to the eigenvector basis and translating to the centre, every conic takes the form\n\n$$\\lambda_1 u^2 + \\lambda_2 v^2 = k,$$\n\nand only the *signs* of $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, k$ matter. Since rotations and translations are exactly the isometries of 1.02, this is a classification up to $E(2)$ — the shapes are genuinely different, not merely differently placed.\n\n| $\\operatorname{sign}(\\lambda_1), \\operatorname{sign}(\\lambda_2)$ | $\\det A$ | Curve |\n|---|---|---|\n| same signs | $> 0$ | **ellipse** (or empty, or a point) |\n| opposite signs | $< 0$ | **hyperbola** (or two crossing lines) |\n| one zero | $= 0$ | **parabola** (or parallel lines, or empty) |\n\nThis notebook derives that table, computes principal axes explicitly, and then does the three-dimensional case where the same logic produces the ellipsoid, the two hyperboloids, and the saddle.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Matrix form, and the two things that classify a conic\n\nWrite the conic homogeneously, absorbing the linear and constant terms into one symmetric $3\\times3$ matrix:\n\n$$Q(x,y) = \\begin{pmatrix}x & y & 1\\end{pmatrix} M \\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad M = \\begin{pmatrix} a_{11} & a_{12} & b_1 \\\\ a_{12} & a_{22} & b_2 \\\\ b_1 & b_2 & c \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe top-left $2\\times2$ block $A$ carries the quadratic part; $M$ carries everything.\n\nTwo determinants do all the classifying:\n\n- **$\\det A$** decides the *type* — ellipse, hyperbola or parabola — because its sign is the product of the eigenvalues.\n- **$\\det M$** decides whether the conic is **non-degenerate**. When $\\det M = 0$ the conic collapses into lines or a point.\n\n**Finding the centre.** A centre is a point of symmetry, and shifting $x \\to x + x_0$ changes the linear term to $2(Ax_0 + b)^{\\mathsf T}x$. So the linear term disappears exactly when\n\n$$A x_0 = -b .$$\n\nIf $A$ is invertible there is a unique centre — ellipses and hyperbolas are *central* conics. If $A$ is singular the equation usually has no solution at all, and that missing centre is precisely what a parabola is: a conic whose centre has run off to infinity. (Stage 2.04 makes that literal.)\n\nBelow we build the machinery and use it on a deliberately awkward rotated conic.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncvec(v) := transpose(matrix(v))$\npt(M)   := args(transpose(M))[1]$\n\n/* Build A, b, c and the extended matrix M from a quadratic expression in x, y */\nconic_parts(Q) := block([A, b, c0],\n  A  : matrix([ coeff(expand(Q), x, 2),      coeff(coeff(expand(Q), x, 1), y, 1)/2 ],\n              [ coeff(coeff(expand(Q), x, 1), y, 1)/2,  coeff(expand(Q), y, 2) ]),\n  b  : [ coeff(subst(y = 0, expand(Q)), x, 1)/2,\n         coeff(subst(x = 0, expand(Q)), y, 1)/2 ],\n  c0 : subst([x = 0, y = 0], expand(Q)),\n  [A, b, c0] )$\n\next_matrix(P) := block([A, b, c0], [A, b, c0] : P,\n  matrix([A[1][1], A[1][2], b[1]],\n         [A[2][1], A[2][2], b[2]],\n         [b[1],    b[2],    c0  ]) )$\n\n/* A rotated, translated ellipse — the coefficients give nothing away by eye */\nQ1 : 5*x^2 - 4*x*y + 8*y^2 - 22*x + 8*y + 5$\nP1 : conic_parts(Q1)$\nM1 : ext_matrix(P1)$\n\nprint(\"A     = \", P1[1], \"     b = \", P1[2], \"   c = \", P1[3])$\nprint(\"det A = \", determinant(P1[1]), \"   det M = \", determinant(M1))$\nprint(\"eigenvalues of A: \", eigenvalues(P1[1])[1])$\nprint(\"→ both positive and det M ≠ 0, so this is a genuine ellipse\")$\n\n/* The centre solves A x₀ = −b */\ncentre : pt( invert(P1[1]) . cvec(-P1[2]) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"centre = \", centre)$\nprint(\"check: linear terms after shifting to the centre —\")$\nQc : expand( subst([x = x + centre[1], y = y + centre[2]], Q1) )$\nprint(\"  coefficient of x: \", coeff(subst(y=0,Qc), x, 1),\n      \"   coefficient of y: \", coeff(subst(x=0,Qc), y, 1))$\nprint(\"  shifted equation: \", Qc)$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "A     =  matrix([5,-2],[-2,8])      b =  [-11,4]    c =  5\ndet A =  36    det M =  -692\neigenvalues of A:  [9,4]\n→ both positive and det M ≠ 0, so this is a genuine ellipse\ncentre =  [20/9,1/18]\ncheck: linear terms after shifting to the centre —\n  coefficient of x:  0    coefficient of y:  0\n  shifted equation:  8*y^2-4*x*y+5*x^2-173/9"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Principal axes: the spectral theorem doing geometry\n\nThe cross term $-4xy$ is not a feature of the ellipse; it is a feature of our coordinates. Removing it means finding the directions in which the quadratic form is \"pure\", and those are the **eigenvectors** of $A$.\n\nThe spectral theorem guarantees this always works for a real symmetric matrix: the eigenvalues are real, and eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are automatically orthogonal. That orthogonality is the whole point — it means the change of basis is a *rotation*, so the shape is genuinely unchanged rather than distorted.\n\nWriting $A = R\\Lambda R^{\\mathsf T}$ with $R \\in SO(2)$ and setting $(u,v)^{\\mathsf T} = R^{\\mathsf T}(x - x_0)$:\n\n$$\\lambda_1 u^2 + \\lambda_2 v^2 = k, \\qquad k = -\\det M / \\det A .$$\n\nFor an ellipse the semi-axes are $\\sqrt{k/\\lambda_1}$ and $\\sqrt{k/\\lambda_2}$, so the **larger eigenvalue gives the shorter axis** — a large $\\lambda$ means the form grows quickly in that direction, so the curve reaches $k$ sooner.\n\nThis is the first place where eigenvalues stop being an algebraic device and start being geometry. It is worth flagging that the pattern recurs, in almost exactly this form, at every later stage:\n\n| Symmetric object | Eigenvalues are | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| quadratic form $A$ | principal axes of a conic | here |\n| Hessian of a function | directions of maximal curvature of a graph | 3.05 |\n| shape operator of a surface | **principal curvatures** $\\kappa_1, \\kappa_2$ | 3.04 |\n| Ricci tensor | principal directions of volume distortion | 4.05 |\n\nThe second fundamental form of a surface is literally a quadratic form, diagonalised in exactly this way. Everything below is rehearsal for that.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "A1 : P1[1]$\n\n/* Eigenvectors, normalised.  Maxima returns them unnormalised. */\nev  : eigenvectors(A1)$\nλs  : ev[1][1]$\nvs  : map(lambda([w], block([u], u : w[1], u/sqrt(u . u))), ev[2])$\nprint(\"eigenvalues  = \", λs)$\nprint(\"eigenvectors = \", vs)$\nprint(\"orthogonal?  v1·v2 = \", ratsimp(vs[1] . vs[2]))$\n\n/* Assemble the rotation R whose columns are the eigenvectors */\nR1 : transpose(matrix(vs[1], vs[2]))$\nif determinant(R1) < 0 then R1 : transpose(matrix(vs[2], vs[1]))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"R = \", R1, \"   det R = \", ratsimp(determinant(R1)))$\nprint(\"RᵀAR = \", ratsimp(transpose(R1) . A1 . R1), \"  — diagonal ✓\")$\n\n/* The constant on the right after shifting and rotating */\nk1 : ratsimp( -determinant(M1)/determinant(A1) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"normal form:  λ1 u² + λ2 v² = k  with k = \", k1)$\nprint(\"semi-axes = \", map(lambda([λ], ratsimp(sqrt(k1/λ))), λs),\n      \"  ≈ \", float(map(lambda([λ], sqrt(k1/λ)), λs)))$\nprint(\"the LARGER eigenvalue \", λs[1], \" gives the SHORTER axis\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "eigenvalues  =  [9,4]\neigenvectors =  [[1/sqrt(5),-(2/sqrt(5))],[2/sqrt(5),1/sqrt(5)]]\northogonal?  v1·v2 =  0\nR =  matrix([1/sqrt(5),2/sqrt(5)],[-(2/sqrt(5)),1/sqrt(5)])    det R =  1\nRᵀAR =  matrix([9,0],[0,4])   — diagonal ✓\nnormal form:  λ1 u² + λ2 v² = k  with k =  173/9\nsemi-axes =  [sqrt(173)/9,sqrt(173)/6]   ≈\n            [1.4614384931073228,2.192157739660984]\nthe LARGER eigenvalue  9  gives the SHORTER axis"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* A complete classifier, driven only by det A, det M and the eigenvalue signs */\nclassify_conic(Q) := block([P, A, b, c0, Mx, dA, dM, λ, k, s],\n  P : conic_parts(Q),  [A, b, c0] : P,\n  Mx : ext_matrix(P),\n  dA : determinant(A),  dM : determinant(Mx),\n  /* normalise so that the quadratic part is \"positive-leaning\" */\n  s  : if mat_trace(A) < 0 or (mat_trace(A) = 0 and dA = 0) then -1 else 1,\n  A : s*A,  Mx : s*Mx,  dM : s^3*dM,\n  λ  : eigenvalues(A)[1],\n  if dA > 0 then (\n    k : -dM/dA,\n    if dM = 0 then \"a single point\"\n    elseif k > 0 then \"ellipse\"\n    else \"empty (no real points)\" )\n  elseif dA < 0 then (\n    if dM = 0 then \"two crossing lines\" else \"hyperbola\" )\n  else ( /* dA = 0: the quadratic part is degenerate */\n    if dM # 0 then \"parabola\"\n    elseif A = zeromatrix(2,2) then \"not a conic (linear)\"\n    else \"two parallel, coincident, or no lines\" ) )$\n\nexamples : [\n  [ x^2 + y^2 - 1,                          \"unit circle\" ],\n  [ 5*x^2 - 4*x*y + 8*y^2 - 22*x + 8*y + 5, \"the rotated ellipse above\" ],\n  [ x^2 - y^2 - 1,                          \"unit hyperbola\" ],\n  [ x*y - 1,                                \"hyperbola, rotated 45°\" ],\n  [ y^2 - x,                                \"parabola\" ],\n  [ x^2 + y^2 + 1,                          \"no real points\" ],\n  [ x^2 + y^2,                              \"one point\" ],\n  [ x^2 - y^2,                              \"a crossing pair\" ],\n  [ x^2 - 1,                                \"a parallel pair\" ] ]$\n\nfor e in examples do block([P, Mx],\n  P : conic_parts(e[1]),  Mx : ext_matrix(P),\n  print(\"det A = \", determinant(P[1]), \",  det M = \", determinant(Mx),\n        \"  →  \", classify_conic(e[1]), \"   [\", e[2], \"]\") )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "det A =  1 ,  det M =  -1   →   ellipse    [ unit circle ]\ndet A =  36 ,  det M =  -692   →   ellipse    [ the rotated ellipse above ]\ndet A =  -1 ,  det M =  1   →   hyperbola    [ unit hyperbola ]\ndet A =  -(1/4) ,  det M =  1/4   →   hyperbola    [ hyperbola, rotated 45° ]\ndet A =  0 ,  det M =  -(1/4)   →   parabola    [ parabola ]\ndet A =  1 ,  det M =  1   →   empty (no real points)    [ no real points ]\ndet A =  1 ,  det M =  0   →   a single point    [ one point ]\ndet A =  -1 ,  det M =  0   →   two crossing lines    [ a crossing pair ]\ndet A =  0 ,  det M =  0   →   two parallel, coincident, or no lines    [\n        a parallel pair ]"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* The rotated ellipse with its principal axes drawn in */\nc0 : float(centre)$\nax1 : float( sqrt(k1/λs[1]) )$   /* along eigenvector vs[1], eigenvalue 9 */\nax2 : float( sqrt(k1/λs[2]) )$   /* along eigenvector vs[2], eigenvalue 4 */\ne1 : float(vs[1])$  e2 : float(vs[2])$\n\nseg(p, d, L) := [discrete, [p[1]-L*d[1], p[1]+L*d[1]], [p[2]-L*d[2], p[2]+L*d[2]]]$\n\nprint(\"centre \", c0, \"   semi-axes \", [ax1, ax2])$\n\nplot2d([ Q1 = 0, seg(c0, e1, ax1), seg(c0, e2, ax2) ],\n       [x, -1, 6], [y, -3, 3],\n       [legend, false], [same_xy, true],\n       [title, \"5x² − 4xy + 8y² − 22x + 8y + 5 = 0, with its principal axes\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "centre  [2.2222222222222223,0.05555555555555555]    semi-axes\n       [1.4614384931073228,2.192157739660984]"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/svg+xml": [
       "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"  standalone=\"no\"?>\n<svg \n width=\"600\" height=\"480\"\n viewBox=\"0 0 600 480\"\n xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"\n xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"\n>\n\n<title>Gnuplot</title>\n<desc>Produced by GNUPLOT 6.0 patchlevel 4 </desc>\n\n<g id=\"gnuplot_canvas\">\n\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"600\" height=\"480\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<defs>\n\n\t<circle id='gpDot' r='0.5' stroke-width='0.5' stroke='currentColor'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt0' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 h2 M0,-1 v2'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt1' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,-1 L1,1 M1,-1 L-1,1'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt2' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M-1,0 L1,0 M0,-1 L0,1 M-1,-1 L1,1 M-1,1 L1,-1'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt3' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<rect id='gpPt4' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' fill='currentColor' x='-1' y='-1' width='2' height='2'/>\n\t<circle id='gpPt5' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' cx='0' cy='0' r='1'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt5' id='gpPt6' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt7' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,-1.33 L-1.33,0.67 L1.33,0.67 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt8' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt7' id='gpPt9' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(180)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt9' id='gpPt10' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt3' id='gpPt11' stroke='currentColor' transform='rotate(45)'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt11' id='gpPt12' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<path id='gpPt13' stroke-width='0.222' stroke='currentColor' d='M0,1.330 L1.265,0.411 L0.782,-1.067 L-0.782,-1.076 L-1.265,0.411 z'/>\n\t<use xlink:href='#gpPt13' id='gpPt14' fill='currentColor' stroke='none'/>\n\t<filter id='textbox' filterUnits='objectBoundingBox' x='0' y='0' height='1' width='1'>\n\t  <feFlood flood-color='whit