  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Quadrics: the same argument, one dimension up\n\nNothing about the method used $n = 2$. For $Q(x) = x^{\\mathsf T}Ax + 2b^{\\mathsf T}x + c$ with $A$ a symmetric $3\\times3$ matrix, the spectral theorem again gives $A = R\\Lambda R^{\\mathsf T}$ with $R \\in SO(3)$, and the normal form is\n\n$$\\lambda_1 u^2 + \\lambda_2 v^2 + \\lambda_3 w^2 = k .$$\n\nThe classification is by the **signature** of $A$ — the counts $(p, q, z)$ of positive, negative and zero eigenvalues — together with the sign of $k$.\n\n| Signature of $A$ | $k$ | Surface |\n|---|---|---|\n| $(3,0,0)$ | $>0$ | **ellipsoid** |\n| $(3,0,0)$ | $<0$ | empty |\n| $(2,1,0)$ | $>0$ | **hyperboloid of one sheet** |\n| $(2,1,0)$ | $<0$ | **hyperboloid of two sheets** |\n| $(2,1,0)$ | $=0$ | elliptic **cone** |\n| $(2,0,1)$ | — | **elliptic paraboloid**, or elliptic cylinder |\n| $(1,1,1)$ | — | **hyperbolic paraboloid** (the saddle), or hyperbolic cylinder |\n\nTwo remarks worth carrying forward.\n\n**One sheet or two, from a single sign.** The hyperboloids of one and two sheets have the *same* matrix $A$ and differ only in the sign of $k$ — the same surface family passing through the cone at $k = 0$. Watching a surface change topology as a parameter crosses zero is a preview of Morse theory.\n\n**The saddle is the model of negative curvature.** The hyperbolic paraboloid $z = x^2 - y^2$ curves up in one direction and down in the other. In 3.05 we compute its Gaussian curvature and find $K < 0$, and in 2.06 the hyperbolic plane turns out to be the surface with $K < 0$ everywhere and constant. The signature $(1,1,\\cdot)$ — one positive, one negative eigenvalue — is exactly what \"negatively curved\" will mean.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Signature of a symmetric matrix: (#positive, #negative, #zero) eigenvalues */\nsignature(A) := block([ev, λ, m, p, n, z],\n  ev : eigenvalues(A), λ : ev[1], m : ev[2],\n  p : 0, n : 0, z : 0,\n  for i : 1 thru length(λ) do\n    if λ[i] > 0 then p : p + m[i]\n    elseif λ[i] < 0 then n : n + m[i]\n    else z : z + m[i],\n  [p, n, z] )$\n\ndg(l) := diag_matrix(l[1], l[2], l[3])$\n\nquadrics : [\n  [ dg([1,1,1]),   1, \"ellipsoid\" ],\n  [ dg([1,1,1]),  -1, \"empty\" ],\n  [ dg([1,1,-1]),  1, \"hyperboloid of one sheet\" ],\n  [ dg([1,1,-1]), -1, \"hyperboloid of two sheets\" ],\n  [ dg([1,1,-1]),  0, \"cone\" ],\n  [ dg([1,1,0]),   1, \"elliptic cylinder (paraboloid, with a linear term)\" ],\n  [ dg([1,-1,0]),  1, \"hyperbolic cylinder (the saddle, with a linear term)\" ] ]$\n\nprint(\"  eigenvalues     signature (p,n,z)    k     surface\")$\nfor q in quadrics do\n  print(\"  \", eigenvalues(q[1])[1], \"     \", signature(q[1]),\n        \"        \", q[2], \"   \", q[3])$\n\n/* The two hyperboloids really are the same A with opposite k */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"x² + y² − z² = k :\")$\nfor kk in [1, 0, -1] do\n  print(\"   k = \", kk, \" → \", if kk > 0 then \"one sheet, connected\"\n                              elseif kk = 0 then \"a cone, the transition\"\n                              else \"two sheets, disconnected\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 7,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "  eigenvalues     signature (p,n,z)    k     surface\n   [1]       [3,0,0]          1     ellipsoid\n   [1]       [3,0,0]          -1     empty\n   [-1,1]       [2,1,0]          1     hyperboloid of one sheet\n   [-1,1]       [2,1,0]          -1     hyperboloid of two sheets\n   [-1,1]       [2,1,0]          0     cone\n   [0,1]       [2,0,1]          1\n  elliptic cylinder (paraboloid, with a linear term)\n   [-1,0,1]       [1,1,1]          1\n  hyperbolic cylinder (the saddle, with a linear term)\nx² + y² − z² = k :\n   k =  1  →  one sheet, connected\n   k =  0  →  a cone, the transition\n   k =  -1  →  two sheets, disconnected"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* The hyperbolic paraboloid z = x² − y²: signature (1,1,·), the model saddle.\n   Up along one principal direction, down along the other — this is what\n   negative Gaussian curvature looks like, and 3.05 computes K for it. */\nplot3d( x^2 - y^2, [x, -2, 2], [y, -2, 2],\n        [legend, false],\n        [title, \"z = x² − y²: the saddle, signature (1,1)\"] )$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 8,
