   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\nThe whole classification, in one line: **write the quadratic as a symmetric matrix and look at the eigenvalues.**\n\n| Step | Tool | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| remove the linear term | solve $Ax_0 = -b$ | find the centre (or discover there isn't one) |\n| remove the cross term | spectral theorem, $A = R\\Lambda R^{\\mathsf T}$ | principal axes |\n| classify | signs of $\\lambda_i$ and of $k$ | the type |\n| detect degeneracy | $\\det M = 0$ | lines, points, or nothing |\n\nFor conics, $\\det A > 0$ gives an ellipse, $\\det A < 0$ a hyperbola, $\\det A = 0$ a parabola. For quadrics the signature $(p,q,z)$ does the same job, and the hyperboloids of one and two sheets are distinguished only by the sign of a constant, passing through a cone as it changes.\n\n## What this notebook was really about\n\nThree ideas will be used constantly later, and this is where they first do work.\n\n**A symmetric matrix is a shape.** The eigenvalues are axis lengths, and their signs are the geometry. Stage 3.04 defines the shape operator of a surface as a symmetric operator, and its eigenvalues are the principal curvatures $\\kappa_1, \\kappa_2$ — the *same* diagonalisation, with the same meaning.\n\n**Signature is the invariant.** Not the eigenvalues themselves — those change under a general change of coordinates — but the *number* of positive, negative and zero ones. Sylvester's law of inertia says that count is basis-independent. In Stage 6.01 the signature $(1,3)$ of a Lorentzian metric is what makes spacetime spacetime.\n\n**Degeneracy is where the interesting things happen.** $\\det A = 0$ was the parabola — the conic whose centre escaped to infinity. $k = 0$ was the cone — the surface where two hyperboloids meet and the topology changes. Vanishing determinants mark the boundaries between regimes, and are where later stages will look for singularities, conjugate points and horizons.\n\n## Next\n\n**1.07 — Euclidean Invariants** closes Stage 1 by asking what the Euclidean group actually preserves, and reframes everything we have done as the study of one group's invariants. That reframing is Klein's Erlangen program, and it is the doorway into Stage 2.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
